Original commit body from 53087a6e432511216a8b2378591cdc22b038a31b:

The section 3a walk's index was keyed (symbol_id, def_line) with
DdgEdge.variable DISCARDED, merging the use-sets of every variable defined on
one line. Reproduced on real source through the real pipeline, not a
hand-built dict:

  6   server = await asyncio.start_server(...)   # the SOURCE
  7   keep = str(server); path = name            # uses server, defines path
  8   os.mkdir(path)                             # the SINK, on `path`

`path` derives from the parameter `name`, so nothing flows from source to
sink. The production DDG emits `server def@6 -> use@7`, `keep def@7 -> use@9`
and `path def@7 -> use@8`; the line-keyed index collapses the last two into
`def_line 7 -> {8, 9}`, the walk steps 6 -> 7 -> 8, hits the sink line, and
reports a dependence that does not exist. A security tool asserting a
precision it did not have is what INV-sadah forbids -- and this sat inside the
fix FOR INV-sadah.

VARIABLE-KEYING ALONE IS NOT THE FIX, which matters because it looks like it
should be. `path` genuinely IS defined at line 7, so separating the index
entries still leaves "which variable defined here inherits from `server`?"
unanswered, and the edge set cannot answer it. The STATEMENT's own
defines/uses can -- measured on the fixture, line 7 yields two rows:

    defines=['keep'] uses=['server']      <- inherits
    defines=['path'] uses=['name']        <- does not

RepoDdg now carries stmt_defuse, collected where populate_def_use_for_cfg had
already run and the values were being thrown away. The walk follows a hop only
when a statement at the use line consumes the tainted variable.

EXACTLY ONE CELL MOVES, and the other two arms are controls. Verdict
composition {ddg: 2, ddg_mixed: 1} -> {ddg: 1, ddg_mixed: 2}: `conflated`
demoted to approximate, `connected` (a real dependence) KEEPS precise,
`disconnected` (no dependence, walk escapes) unchanged. RECALL IS UNTOUCHED --
all three flows still reported, because section 3a confirms and never refutes,
so this costs precision-of-label and never a finding.

THE TEST NAMED FOR THE LABEL NEVER ASSERTED THE LABEL.
test_ddg_labels_a_data_connected_flow_as_precise contained "precise" only in
its name and docstring; its sole assertion was recall. The label section 3a
exists to earn had NO test anywhere in the corpus, which is why an unearned one
shipped in #188/#192 and survived three PRs. It now asserts both arms.

MISSING STATEMENT DATA DEGRADES SAFELY, and that is pinned. A caller that omits
stmt_defuse -- or a language whose def/use pass annotates no statements --
cannot justify a hop, so the finding falls back to approximate rather than
silently confirming. A companion test asserts the flow is still reported and
only the label moves. The alternative reading ("no statement data, so assume
the hop is fine") is precisely what produced the unearned label.

A FIFTH PROPERTY JOINS THE PER-LANGUAGE DDG WIRING GATE. A language emitting
edges but annotating no statements would lose every confirmation SILENTLY --
findings keep arriving, only the label quietly degrades -- which is the
ADR-0017 inertness shape. Property (4) asserts the edges exist; (5) asserts
they can be interpreted. All four registered languages pass; Go was checked
explicitly before this landed, since a Go-shaped gap is invisible from Python
fixtures.

ALSO: the INV-lupav guard added in #199 is now UNREACHABLE and marked so. Seeds
come from defs_at, built from the same edges as ddg_uses, and every frontier
append is membership-tested, so a popped pair always has uses. The protection
did not disappear -- it moved to SEEDING, the earlier and more precise place
for it. The branch is kept as defensive (without it, a future unvalidated
append gets a TypeError on `uses & targets` instead of the correct conservative
answer) and its comment rewritten, because the old one now asserted something
false about the present.

